  "before_interfaces": {
    "def-mean-zero-square-integrable-space": [
      "## Definition\n\nLet $P$ be a probability measure on $(\\mathcal X,\\mathcal A)$. Here $L^2(P)$ is the real space of square-integrable measurable functions modulo equality $P$-almost surely, with its usual quotient norm ([[def-l-p-space-as-a-quotient-by-null-functions]], [[thm-the-l-p-norm-descends-to-the-quotient-and-makes-l-p-a-normed-space]]). For $f\\in L^2(P)$, the integral $Pf:=\\int f\\,dP$ is finite and independent of the representative: Cauchy–Schwarz on the probability space gives $\\int|f|\\,dP\\le\\|f\\|_2$, and the expectation of integrable representatives is invariant under almost-sure equality ([[cor-cauchy-schwarz-inequality-for-l-two]], [[lem-expectation-is-independent-of-the-ae-representative]]).\n\nDefine the **mean-zero square-integrable space** by\n\n$$L^2_0(P):=\\{f\\in L^2(P):Pf=0\\}.$$\n\nIt carries the norm inherited from $L^2(P)$. Countable Choice is not needed for this definition; a later local Hilbert-space argument uses it only to inherit completeness from the library's general $L^2$ Hilbert-space result."
    ],
    "def-dqm-submodel-through-a-law": [
      "## Definition\n\nLet $\\mathcal M$ be a model of probability measures on a measurable space $(\\mathcal X,\\mathcal A)$, let $\\epsilon>0$, and let $t\\mapsto P_t\\in\\mathcal M$ be a path for $0\\le t<\\epsilon$ with $P_0=P$. It is **dominated by a common measure** when one supplied sigma-finite positive measure $\\mu$ satisfies $P_t\\ll\\mu$ for every $t$ ([[def-dominated-statistical-experiment]]). Write $p_t=dP_t/d\\mu$ and $p=p_0$ for simultaneously supplied nonnegative density versions; the existence and normalization of each density are the Radon–Nikodym properties in [[def-likelihood-density]] and [[thm-radon-nikodym-density-exists-and-is-unique-up-to-almost-everywhere-equality]]. The Axiom of Choice is the library convention for selecting such a family of versions along a path ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]). The DQM property below is unchanged by replacing any $p_t$ by another version, since each $L^2(\\mu)$ norm is unchanged by a $\\mu$-null modification.\n\nThe path is **differentiable in quadratic mean at $0$**, with **score** $s\\in L^2(P)$, when\n\n$$\\left\\|\\sqrt{p_t}-\\sqrt p-\\frac{t}{2}s\\sqrt p\\right\\|_{L^2(\\mu)}=o(t)\\qquad(t\\downarrow0).$$\n\nHere $o(t)$ means that the displayed norm divided by $t$ tends to $0$ as $t\\downarrow0$; $L^2(P)$ and $L^2(\\mu)$ are quotient spaces modulo almost-everywhere equality ([[def-l-p-space-as-a-quotient-by-null-functions]]). The definition is directional. A two-sided regular submodel qualifies by restricting it to $t\\ge0$. No mean-zero condition is assumed in this definition."
    ],
    "thm-mean-zero-square-integrable-space-is-hilbert": [
      "## Statement\n\nAssume Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). For any probability measure $P$, the space $L^2_0(P)$ of [[def-mean-zero-square-integrable-space]] is a closed real linear subspace of real $L^2(P)$ with pairing $\\langle f,g\\rangle_P:=\\int fg\\,dP$; consequently it is a real Hilbert space ([[def-hilbert-space]]). The only choice use is the Countable Choice assumption already required by the general $L^2(P)$ completeness supplier."
    ],
    "thm-dqm-score-mean-zero": [
      "## Statement\n\nAssume the density-version convention for the path in [[def-dqm-submodel-through-a-law]]. If $s\\in L^2(P)$ is a DQM score, then $Ps=0$, so $s\\in L^2_0(P)$ ([[def-mean-zero-square-integrable-space]]). Moreover, any two scores satisfying the same DQM expansion agree $P$-almost surely. The normalization argument itself uses no choice beyond the density versions already supplied in the definition."
    ],
    "def-tangent-set": [
      "## Definition\n\nFix a model $\\mathcal M$ containing a law $P$ and designate a collection of allowed submodels through $P$. Each allowed submodel is a one-sided DQM path as in [[def-dqm-submodel-through-a-law]]. Its score is the $P$-almost-sure equivalence class proved unique by [[thm-dqm-score-mean-zero]], and it belongs to $L^2_0(P)$ ([[def-mean-zero-square-integrable-space]]).\n\nThe **tangent set** $\\dot{\\mathcal M}(P)$ is the set of scores of the designated paths. The model specification determines which paths are allowed. In particular, the tangent set need not be a linear space: it may be a **cone**, meaning it is closed under multiplication by nonnegative real scalars, while failing closure under negative scalars. One-sided restrictions can produce this behavior. The closed linear span of the tangent set is a separate construction."
    ],
    "def-tangent-space": [
      "## Definition\n\nAssume Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]) so that $L^2_0(P)$ is the real Hilbert space established by [[thm-mean-zero-square-integrable-space-is-hilbert]]. For a tangent set $\\dot{\\mathcal M}(P)\\subseteq L^2_0(P)$, its **linear span** is the set of all finite real linear combinations of elements of $\\dot{\\mathcal M}(P)$; the span of the empty set is $\\{0\\}$. Its **tangent space** $\\mathcal T(P)$ is the closure of that span in the $L^2(P)$ norm. The closure consists of limits of sequences from the span, equivalently of points every positive-radius norm ball around which meets the span.\n\nThe tangent set itself is retained separately and may be a cone rather than a vector space."
    ],
    "thm-tangent-space-is-closed-linear-subspace": [
      "## Statement\n\nAssume Countable Choice. Let $\\dot{\\mathcal M}(P)$ be any tangent set in the real Hilbert space $L^2_0(P)$. Its tangent space $\\mathcal T(P)$, defined as the norm closure of its finite real linear span ([[def-tangent-space]]), is a closed linear subspace of $L^2_0(P)$ ([[def-linear-subspace]])."
    ],
    "def-nuisance-tangent-space": [
      "## Definition\n\nLet $\\mathcal M$ be a model through $P$, let $\\psi:\\mathcal M\\to\\mathbb R^k$ be a specified finite-dimensional parameter-of-interest functional, and let the model designate its nuisance submodels. A **nuisance submodel** is a designated allowed DQM path $t\\mapsto P_t$ through $P$ for which the right derivative $\\lim_{t\\downarrow0}(\\psi(P_t)-\\psi(P))/t$ exists and is the zero vector in $\\mathbb R^k$. Its score belongs to the tangent set $\\dot{\\mathcal M}(P)$ ([[def-tangent-set]]).\n\nThe **nuisance tangent space** $\\mathcal T_\\eta(P)$ is the norm closure in $L^2_0(P)$ of the finite real linear span of scores of nuisance submodels. It is a closed linear subspace by [[def-tangent-space]] and [[thm-tangent-space-is-closed-linear-subspace]]. The designated nuisance submodels are part of the model specification; this definition does not assert that every path with zero first-order target derivative is nuisance unless the model designates it as such."
    ],
    "def-nuisance-tangent-orthogonal-complement": [
      "## Definition\n\nAssume Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]), so that $L^2_0(P)$ is the real Hilbert space of [[thm-mean-zero-square-integrable-space-is-hilbert]]. The **orthogonal complement of the nuisance tangent space within $L^2_0(P)$** is\n\n$$\\mathcal T_\\eta(P)^\\perp:=\\{h\\in L^2_0(P):\\langle h,g\\rangle_P=0\\text{ for every }g\\in\\mathcal T_\\eta(P)\\},\\qquad \\langle h,g\\rangle_P:=P(hg).$$\n\nThis is the ordinary orthogonal complement from [[def-orthogonality-and-orthogonal-complement]] applied in the mean-zero Hilbert space, not in all of $L^2(P)$. It is a closed linear subspace by [[lem-orthogonal-complement-is-closed]]."
    ],
    "ex-bounded-scores-generate-unrestricted-tangent-space": [],
    "ex-regular-bernoulli-submodel-dqm-score": [],
    "ex-boundary-score-cone-and-linear-tangent-space": []
  }
